== Epistle to the Reader (1539)

\[Prefixed to the second edition, published at Strasburg \[Strasbourg\] in 1539.\]

#smallcaps[In] the first edition of this work I had no expectation of the success which God, in his goodness, has been pleased to give it, and so for the most part I carried out my task in a cursory way, as is usual with small works. But when I saw that almost all the godly had received it with a favor which I had never dared to wish for, far less to hope for, I was sincerely aware that I had received much more than I deserved. I thought I would be very ungrateful if I did not try, at least according to my humble ability, to respond to the great kindness which had been shown to me, and which of its own accord urged me to diligence. I therefore ask no other favor from studious readers for my new work than the one they have already given me beyond my merits. I feel so much in their debt that I will be satisfied if I am thought not to have made a poor return for the gratitude I owe. I would have made this return much earlier, had not the Lord, for almost two whole years, tried me in extraordinary ways. But it is soon enough if it is good enough. I will think it has appeared in good time when I see that it produces some fruit for the Church of God. I may add that my aim in this work was to prepare and train students of theology for the study of the Sacred Volume, so that they might both have an easy introduction to it and be able to go on in it with an unfaltering step. For I have tried to give such a summary of religion in all its parts, and have arranged it in such an order, that anyone who rightly grasps it will find it not difficult to determine both what he ought chiefly to look for in Scripture, and also to what end he ought to relate whatever it contains. Having thus, as it were, paved the way, I will not find it necessary, in any commentaries on Scripture which I may publish later, to enter into long discussions of doctrine or to enlarge on doctrinal topics, and so I will always keep them brief. In this way the godly reader will be spared much trouble and weariness, provided he comes equipped with a knowledge of the present work as an essential prerequisite. As my Commentary on the Epistle to the Romans will give a sample of this plan, I would much rather let it speak for itself than describe it in words. Farewell, dear reader, and if you gain any fruit from my labors, help me with your prayers to the Lord.

Strasbourg, 1#emph[st August] 1539.
